fof_version: '1.0'
type: enemy
id: chicken
name: Chicken
ruleset:
  modules:
  - id: base
    version: 1.0.0
enemy:
  # v0.3: Mook, attack -1, rating 1 (the baseline).
  level: 1
  role: mook
  armor: 0
  morale: 2
  weapon: Beak, claws, and opinions
  damage: 1
  attack: -1
  wants: To be somewhere else. It has not decided where.
  special: 'UNDERFOOT — when the chicken hits, the MM may skip the damage and put it in someone''s
    face instead: that character''s next roll this exchange is Hard.'
  when_bloodied: null
  tells: It has been making the same noise since you came in, and the noise has been getting
    louder.
  breaks: Wanders off the moment nothing is chasing it, and turns up later somewhere nobody
    can explain.
  twists:
  - It is not one chicken. It has never been one chicken.
  - It belongs to someone nearby, who is on the way and wants to know what you are doing to
    it.
  - It is sitting on the thing the party came for, and will not move for anything short of
    being picked up.
  - It got into the party's supplies first. Somebody's rations are now mostly chicken's.
  - It is the only calm thing in the room, which is somehow worse.
  - It follows the party out, and keeps following them for the rest of the session.
  nastier: There is no nastier chicken. There is only more chicken.
  description: >-
    A chicken. Technically alive. Technically a threat, in the sense that it occupies space
    and has opinions about personal boundaries. Attacks by pecking at ankles or flapping directly
    into someone's face at the worst possible moment. Not malevolent. Just a chicken.
  notes: >-
    The canonical weakest creature in the game, and the baseline the rest of the Bestiary
    is read against. Level 1 Mook with two overrides, both deliberate: attack −1 and damage
    1, one step under the level-1 Mook floor (attack 0, damage 2), so that nothing else in
    the book can be weaker than the chicken.


    Like every Mook it drops to any hit. Its erratic movement is not evasion. A chicken does
    not dodge. It simply goes somewhere unpredictable. This is functionally the same thing
    and somehow more annoying.


    Morale 2: at any morale trigger it leaves unless the MM rolls snake eyes.


    MMs are encouraged to have the chicken survive the encounter through sheer irrelevance
    and wander off at the end. It was never really part of this.


    Do not give the chicken a name. If the party gives it a name, that is entirely their fault
    and the MM should remember it forever.


    Appears: alone, in pairs, or in a genuinely alarming quantity.
  reskin_of: null
